Subject: [PATCH 0004] Hash a pointer by its address, and assert the address width
Upstream: CPython 3.13.7
Upstream-Archive: https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.13.7/Python-3.13.7.tgz
Upstream-SHA256: 6c9d80839cfa20024f34d9a6dd31ae2a9cd97ff5e980e969209746037a5153b2
Build-Variants: capstone64 domain (musl)
Depends-On: cpython-3.13.7-0001-address-width.patch
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

_Py_HashPointerRaw (Include/internal/pycore_pyhash.h) converts the pointer to
uintptr_t, rotates it by 4 and returns it, after asserting
sizeof(uintptr_t) == sizeof(ptr). On capstone64 that is 8 against 16 and the
assert fails in every core file (161 in the fifth survey). The assert guards
against losing bits the hash needs; the hash needs the address, which is all
the conversion carries and all that distinguishes two live objects. The rotate
already uses sizeof(uintptr_t). The assert now checks the address width, which
is the same condition wherever the two sizes agree.

This patch does not make any pointer<->integer ROUND TRIP safe. The hash is
never converted back; code that does convert back is a separate finding.

--- a/Include/internal/pycore_pyhash.h
+++ b/Include/internal/pycore_pyhash.h
@@ -9,8 +9,11 @@
 static inline Py_hash_t
 _Py_HashPointerRaw(const void *ptr)
 {
+    // The hash is of the address. Where a pointer is a capability the
+    // conversion keeps only the address, which is what identity means here:
+    // two live objects never share one.
     uintptr_t x = (uintptr_t)ptr;
-    Py_BUILD_ASSERT(sizeof(x) == sizeof(ptr));
+    Py_BUILD_ASSERT(sizeof(x) == _Py_SIZEOF_ADDRESS);
 
     // Bottom 3 or 4 bits are likely to be 0; rotate x by 4 to the right
     // to avoid excessive hash collisions for dicts and sets.
